I apologize for the empty response earlier. Here's a revised attempt to address your original question:

**Is it possible to create a class in Java that extends another class but does not inherit any of its properties?**

Yes, it is possible to create a class in Java that extends another class but does not inherit any of its properties. Inheritance in Java allows you to create a new class based on an existing class, and the new class can either extend or implement the existing class.

Here's an example:

```java
// Comment.java
public abstract class Comment {
    public String data;

    public Comment(String data) {
        this.data = data;
    }

    public abstract void printComment();
}

// XmlDeclaration.java
public class XmlDeclaration extends Comment {
    @Override
    public void printComment() {
        System.out.println("This is an XML declaration.");
    }
}
```

In this example, the `XmlDeclaration` class extends the `Comment` class and does not inherit any properties from it. The `XmlDeclaration` class only inherits the `data` field from the `Comment` class.

If you want to ensure that all subclasses of a certain class do not inherit any properties from the parent class, you can use interfaces instead.

```java
// Comment.java
public interface Comment {
    void printComment();
}

// XmlDeclaration.java
public class XmlDeclaration implements Comment {
    @Override
    public void printComment() {
        System.out.println("This is an XML declaration.");
    }
}
```

In this case, the `XmlDeclaration` class implements the `Comment` interface and does not inherit any properties from it.

Alternatively, you can use a technique called "diamond inheritance" or "multiple inheritance" to achieve this. However, diamond inheritance is only possible in Java 10 and later versions, due to the introduction of the `extends` clause with multiple type parameters.

```java
// Comment.java
public abstract class Comment {
    public String data;

    public Comment(String data) {
        this.data = data;
    }

    public abstract void printComment();
}

// XmlDeclaration.java
public class XmlDeclaration extends Comment<String, Void> {
    @Override
    public void printComment() {
        System.out.println("This is an XML declaration.");
    }
}
```

In this example, the `XmlDeclaration` class extends the `Comment` class with two type parameters: `String` and `Void`. This allows you to inherit only the methods from the `Comment` class that do not have any type parameters.
